# wmbus-toolkit configuration
#
# Copy to meters.conf and fill in your own data. meters.conf is ignored by
# git, so real meter data never ends up in the repository.
#
# Format: one line per meter, fields comma-separated, key=value.
# Blank lines and # are ignored.
#
# Required:  id        - meter id as shown by wmbusmeters (8 digits)
# Optional:  name      - free name for the output
#            serial    - printed serial number from the label
#            model     - model/type number from the label
#            installed - install / commissioning date (YYYY-MM-DD, optional
#                        time as YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM, or DD.MM.YYYY)
#            mfct      - FLAG manufacturer code (e.g. QDS, KAM); usually auto-
#                        detected from the telegrams, set only to override
#            version   - meter version byte (2 hex); also auto-detected
#            key       - if already known, 32 hex chars. keygen writes it first,
#                        ahead of every guess (category `known`); keycheck
#                        --meters meters.conf uses it directly, no keyfile needed
#
# serial, model, installed and mfct feed the candidate generator with more
# derived keys. If absent, only id-based and generic candidates are built.
# keygen reads the install date, manufacturer and version straight from the
# meter's own telegrams with --from-csv telegrams.csv, so those fields are
# optional. With `wmbus-keygen --from-csv ... --all-meters` you do not even need
# a meters.conf: every meter seen on the air gets its own candidate set.

# Examples with mock data (commented out - replace with your own lines):

# id=12345678, name=MeterA, serial=1234567890
# id=23456789, name=MeterB, serial=2345678901, model=ABC1234, installed=2021-03-15
# id=34567890, name=MeterC, model=XYZ5678, key=0123456789ABCDEF0123456789ABCDEF
